"Nontransitive and Balanced Sets of Dice - Harvard Math (https://www.math.harvard.edu/event/nontransitive-and-balanced-sets-of-dice/)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.math.harvard.edu/event/nontransitive-and-balanced-sets-of-dice/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 56\nL0:   * cite0†Home L1:   * cite1†About L2:   * cite2†People L3:   * cite3†Events L4:   * cite4†Community†sites.harvard.edu L5:   * cite5†Undergraduate L6:   * cite6†Graduate L7:   * cite7†Jobs L8:   * cite8†Resources L9: \nL10: cite9†Support Harvard Math L11: \nL12: [Input: Search …] [Input]\nL13: # Nontransitive and Balanced Sets of Dice\nL14: \nL15: MATH TABLE\nL16: \nL17: When: November 20, 2024\nL18: \nL19: 4:30 pm - 5:30 pm\nL20: \nL21: Where: Science Center 507\nL22: \nL23: Address: 1 Oxford Street, Cambridge, MA 02138, United States\nL24: \nL25: Speaker: Josh Rooney - Harvard\nL26: Let’s recall the game “rock-paper-scissors.” Although rock beats scissors and scissors beats paper, rock does not beat paper. We call this property “nontransitivity.” Does this same idea translate to dice? In 1970, Martin Gardner popularized a set of 4 dice (A,B,C,D) “Efron dice” in which P(B>A)= P(C>B) = P(D>C) = P(A>D) = 2/3.\nL27: Since these probabilities are greater than 1/2, this set of dice is nontransitive, and since the probabilities are equal, we will call the set “balanced.” The dominance probability, 2/3 in this case, that is possible for such a set of dice is dependent on the number of dice in the set. In this talk, we will fully classify all such probabilities for a set of n nontransitive and balanced dice. This talk will aim to be accessible to all undergraduates.\nL28:   *     * cite10†Announcements L29:     * cite11†Recent News L30: \nL31: * * *\nL32: \nL33:   * cite12†Announcements Archive L34:   * cite13†Conference Archive L35:   * cite14†Events Archive L36:   * cite15